Assume successful process creation and normal exits. How many times does this print `x`?

```c
int main(void) {
  /* Every surviving process forks */
  /* again. */
  fork();
  fork();
  printf("x\n");
  return 0;
}
```

Assume successful process creation and normal exits. What does this print?

```c
int x = 10;
pid_t p = fork();
if (p == 0) {
  /* Only child private memory */
  /* changes. */
  x = x * 2;
  printf("c %d\n", x);
  exit(0);
}
/* Wait makes the output order */
/* predictable. */
wait(NULL);
printf("p %d\n", x);
```

Assume successful fork, normal exit, conventional terminal line buffering or pipe full buffering, and one complete write per flush. What does this print when run on a terminal, and when piped into `cat`?

```c
printf("a\n");
/* An unflushed buffer is copied too. */
fork();
printf("b\n");
```

Assume successful process creation and normal exits. How many lines contain `A`, and how many `B`?

```c
/* A zero left operand skips the */
/* right. */
if (fork() && fork())
  printf("A\n");
else
  printf("B\n");
```
